#
# The KS-19 boot ruleset for macOS: what protects the host between the network
# stack coming up and the system extension being activated.
#
# Installed at  /etc/twinvpn/pf.anchor  (ADR-0016 §11.5's macOS row).
# Loaded into anchor  "twinvpn"  from /etc/pf.conf (ADR-0012 §11.6's macOS row:
# "`pf` anchor `twinvpn` (both families) + `NEPacketTunnelNetworkSettings`
# carrying `IPv4Settings` and `IPv6Settings`").
# Applied by  com.twinvpn.ksd  with RunAtLoad=true — the package-owned boot
# artifact of ADR-0016 PS-7, which is deliberately NOT the authority.
#
# ADR-0012 §11.8's boot row:
#   boot: OS applies boot ruleset -> network stack comes up -> agent starts
#         (the deny predates the first packet the host can emit)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- WHAT THIS IS
#
# `RULESET_BLOCKED` for a host that has **no contract yet**, so the Tier-1
# protected scope is not yet known. It denies the one thing protected on every
# TwinVPN host regardless of contract: the overlay address space itself —
# ADR-0010 §11.1's IPv4 `100.64.0.0/10` (RFC 6598) and the pinned product ULA
# `fd7c:9e5d:2a10::/48` (AP-1, "a pinned constant, identical in every build").
#
# It deliberately does **not** default-deny all egress, and it deliberately does
# **not** contain all DNS. ADR-0012 **KS-3a**: the traffic-class table is
# "exhaustive over the Tier-1 protected set, which is mode-dependent; traffic
# outside that set is not governed by this table and is not dropped by it." A
# boot ruleset that denied everything would take the host off the network before
# the authority could tell it not to — including the ssh session an operator
# needs to fix it. This mirrors `shells/linux/packaging/killswitch.nft` exactly.
#
# The authority **replaces** this content at runtime by loading its own rules
# into the same anchor with `pfctl -a twinvpn -f -`, which pf applies as one
# transaction — KS-17's atomic swap, never a remove-then-add (KS-23).
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------ KS-5
#
# Every IPv4 rule below has its IPv6 counterpart on the adjacent line. pf has no
# `inet`+`inet6` union address family, so — unlike nftables' single `inet` table
# — the structural guarantee cannot come from "there is no second object to
# forget". It comes instead from the adapter's renderer (`pf.rs`
# `emit_scope_drop`), which is one function emitting both families and has no
# call site that can emit one without the other. THIS FILE IS HAND-WRITTEN AND
# THEREFORE DOES NOT HAVE THAT GUARANTEE; it has a test in the adapter that
# parses it and asserts both families are present. Stated, not assumed.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------- LABELS
#
# `label` is pf's per-rule counter, read back with `pfctl -a twinvpn -s labels`.
# It is the macOS equivalent of the nftables named counter, and it is what makes
# the ProtectionAssertion a QUERY of the kernel rather than a belief (ADR-0015
# §11.6 rule 1, ownership.md §8 W-24). Nothing here is derived from the fact
# that a load call returned success.
#
# UNVERIFIED: the exact column layout of `pfctl -s labels` on Apple's pf fork
# (OpenBSD 4.x-derived) has not been observed on a Mac by this domain. The
# adapter's parser is written against the OpenBSD 4.x format
# (`name evaluations packets bytes ...`) and tolerates trailing columns.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ NO LOGGING
#
# No rule carries `log`. A `log` rule copies matched packets to `pflog0`, where
# `tcpdump` can read them — that is a tunnel-payload capture channel, and
# ADR-0015 §11.4 forbids one. Counters are sufficient for the canary.
#

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tables. `persist` keeps a table that is momentarily empty from being garbage
# collected, which is what makes an empty portal table representable at all.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The Tier-1 baseline protected set. A FLOOR beneath a real contract's scope,
# never a substitute for it: on a full-tunnel host the protected set is
# everything, and two prefixes under-cover it. The authority re-renders the
# applied contract's own scope into the runtime anchor.
table <twinvpn_protected_v4> persist { 100.64.0.0/10 }
table <twinvpn_protected_v6> persist { fd7c:9e5d:2a10::/48 }

# ADR-0012 KS-15's captive-portal grant set: "a `pf` table entry with expiry
# sweep", lifetime <= 300 s. Declared `persist` and EMPTY here — the boot
# artifact never grants a portal, and the expiry sweep is the authority's, not
# this file's. Present so the runtime anchor's rules have a table to reference
# rather than failing to parse on a fresh host.
table <twinvpn_portal> persist

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The read-back's answer, held by pf itself (CB-6, W-24).
#
# pf has no named counters, so where nftables carries the posture and the
# generation as `counter posture_blocked` / `counter gen_<n>` objects, macOS
# carries them as MARKER TABLES. `pfctl -a twinvpn -s Tables` lists them
# structurally — a table name is not free text a parser has to guess at, which
# is the same reason the Linux adapter encodes state as a counter rather than as
# a rule comment. They are kernel-resident so they survive a core crash (CB-6),
# they are removed with the anchor by an uninstall, and they are readable by a
# privileged local unblock command with the authority absent (KS-20a).
#
# No members and no references, by design. `persist` is therefore load-bearing:
# without it pf discards a table that no rule mentions, and the markers would
# vanish the moment they were loaded.
#
# The adapter's `pfread::parse_tables` reads exactly these names.
#
# Generation 0 and posture BLOCKED are the TRUTHFUL boot-time answer: the KS-19
# artifact IS the blocked ruleset (KS-17 — two rulesets, never zero), and no
# contract has been applied. The authority replaces the whole anchor on its
# first apply, in one `pfctl -a twinvpn -f -` transaction, which is KS-23's
# atomic swap rather than a remove-then-add.
#
# `tv_posture_protected` is deliberately ABSENT and must stay absent: both
# posture tables present at once is a ruleset nobody rendered, and the parser
# resolves that ambiguity to `Blocked` — the fail-closed direction.
#
# The `_n1` suffixes are the SCOPE CARDINALITY per family — R-6's detector. The
# posture says what was intended; these say how many prefixes the Tier-2 deny
# actually covers, so that "BLOCKED over 1 prefix" and "BLOCKED over nothing"
# are different readings rather than the same one. This anchor's deny covers
# exactly one prefix in each family — `100.64.0.0/10` in <twinvpn_protected_v4>
# and `fd7c:9e5d:2a10::/48` in <twinvpn_protected_v6> — so both are `n1`. **If a
# prefix is ever added to or removed from either table above, these two names
# must change with it**: a wrong count is worse than no count, because it makes
# a shrinking scope invisible to the read-back that exists to catch it.
table <tv_posture_blocked> persist
table <tv_gen_0> persist
table <tv_scope4_n1> persist
table <tv_scope6_n1> persist

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Filtering.
#
# pf is LAST-MATCH-WINS unless a rule carries `quick`. Every rule here carries
# `quick` and the order below IS the precedence, so that reading the file top to
# bottom is reading the decision procedure. A non-`quick` mix would make the
# disposition of a packet depend on rules further down that a reader has not got
# to yet, which is how a "fail-closed" ruleset comes to permit something.
#
# `set skip on lo0` is NOT used: `set` statements are only legal in the main
# ruleset, never inside an anchor. Class 8 is an explicit pass instead.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Class 8 — loopback. Permitted; cannot egress by construction. `no state`
# because a state entry per loopback flow is pure overhead on a path that
# cannot leave the host.
pass quick on lo0 all no state label "twinvpn.loopback"

# Class 7 — the bootstrap exception (KS-9). ADR-0012 §11.5's macOS predicate is
# verbatim "`pf` anchor keyed to the tunnel provider's owning uid plus the
# provider's socket set". The uid half is expressible here; the socket-set half
# is NOT — pf has no socket-identity match — so this file implements the
# WEAKER half alone and says so. The authority's runtime anchor is where the
# registered-socket scoping of KS-9(2) lives, and KS-9a forbids that
# registration being an IPC operation.
#
# UNVERIFIED: the `user` keyword. It exists in OpenBSD pf and FreeBSD pf and
# matches only LOCALLY ORIGINATED packets — which is a property KS-2 wants, since
# a forwarded packet has no owning uid and therefore cannot match this rule and
# cannot borrow the exemption. Whether Apple's pf fork retained `user` has not
# been checked on a Mac by this domain. If it did not, this rule fails to parse,
# `pfctl -f` rejects the whole anchor, and the KS-19 artifact does not load at
# all — which is why the installer validates with `pfctl -n -f` before
# committing (see install.sh).
#
# `_twinvpn` is the service account of ADR-0016 §11.7's macOS row, created by
# the installer. It is spelled as a NAME here; pf resolves it at parse time, so
# the anchor must be loaded after the account exists.
pass out quick inet  from any to any user _twinvpn keep state label "twinvpn.exempt.v4"
pass out quick inet6 from any to any user _twinvpn keep state label "twinvpn.exempt.v6"

# Class 5 — underlay DHCP / DHCPv6 / ND / RA. Permitted as link-local control
# traffic only. ADR-0010 §11.5(5) is explicit that blocking them breaks the
# underlay itself. There is no overlay interface at boot, so these are not
# qualified `on ! utunN`; the authority's runtime anchor scopes them off the
# overlay so they cannot become a hole in the tunnel.
pass out quick inet  proto udp from any port 68  to any port 67  keep state label "twinvpn.underlay.dhcp4"
pass out quick inet6 proto udp from any port 546 to any port 547 keep state label "twinvpn.underlay.dhcp6"
# ICMPv6 133-137: router solicit/advert, neighbour solicit/advert, redirect.
# Written NUMERICALLY rather than with pf's symbolic names (`routersol`,
# `routeradv`, `neighbrsol`, `neighbradv`, `redir`) because the symbolic set
# differs between pf forks and a name Apple's fork does not know is a parse
# error that takes the whole boot artifact with it. The numbers are RFC 4443's
# and do not vary.
pass out quick inet6 proto icmp6 icmp6-type { 133, 134, 135, 136, 137 } keep state label "twinvpn.underlay.nd"

# Class 9 — link-local unicast, both families. Not routable off-link by
# definition; required for ND and IPv4LL.
pass out quick inet  from any to 169.254.0.0/16 keep state label "twinvpn.linklocal.v4"
pass out quick inet6 from any to fe80::/10      keep state label "twinvpn.linklocal.v6"

# ---- the Tier-2 deny ------------------------------------------------------
#
# Class 6, the DNS half, gets its OWN rule and its OWN label ahead of the
# general deny — not because it is a different disposition, but because
# ADR-0011 §11.12's negative canary asks a different question from ADR-0012's
# ("was my off-tunnel DNS query dropped" rather than "was my off-tunnel
# protected packet dropped") and therefore needs its own number.
#
# It is SCOPED TO THE PROTECTED TABLES, not to every destination. A blanket
# `block ... port 53` in the boot artifact would take the host off name
# resolution entirely before the authority exists, which is exactly the
# KS-3a over-reading. Full class-6 containment — all DNS in full-tunnel mode —
# is mode-dependent and therefore belongs to the authority's runtime anchor,
# where the mode is known. `shells/linux/packaging/killswitch.nft` draws the
# same line and for the same reason.
block drop out quick inet  proto { tcp, udp } from any to <twinvpn_protected_v4> port { 53, 853 } label "twinvpn.deny.dns"
block drop out quick inet6 proto { tcp, udp } from any to <twinvpn_protected_v6> port { 53, 853 } label "twinvpn.deny.dns"

# Classes 1-3. No interface is exempt: no overlay interface exists yet. Both
# families, adjacent, as KS-5 requires.
block drop out quick inet  from any to <twinvpn_protected_v4> label "twinvpn.deny.v4"
block drop out quick inet6 from any to <twinvpn_protected_v6> label "twinvpn.deny.v6"

# KS-2 — the forwarding path. "Forwarded traffic is protected by the same Tier-2
# rule and is NEVER eligible for any exemption in §11.2."
#
# **This is satisfied, but not by a rule of its own, and the difference is worth
# reading.** pf has no hook distinct from `out` for forwarded packets: a
# forwarded packet traverses `out` on its egress interface and therefore already
# meets the two `block drop out quick ... <twinvpn_protected_*>` rules above.
# What makes the exemption unreachable for it is that pf's `user` keyword
# matches only LOCALLY ORIGINATED traffic — a forwarded packet has no owning
# uid, so it cannot match `user _twinvpn` and cannot borrow class 7. That is a
# structural property of pf rather than discipline in this file.
#
# The labels `twinvpn.deny.fwd.v4` / `twinvpn.deny.fwd.v6` are therefore NOT
# emitted here. They belong to the authority's runtime anchor, where the overlay
# interface exists and `on ! utunN` can separate the two paths so the canary can
# tell "we dropped a forwarded protected packet" from "we dropped a locally
# originated one".
#
# **A rule that was written and then deliberately removed, recorded so it is not
# re-added:** an inbound counterpart
# (`block drop in quick inet from any to <twinvpn_protected_v4>`) would give the
# forwarding path its own label at boot. It is wrong. `100.64.0.0/10` is RFC 6598
# *carrier* shared address space, and a Mac behind a CGNAT ISP legitimately holds
# an address in it — so an inbound deny over that table would black-hole the
# host's own underlay before the authority ever ran. `shells/linux/packaging/
# killswitch.nft` hooks `output` and `forward` and deliberately never `input`,
# for the same reason.
